We have defined a Vehicle class that has a method drive() which prints "Normal Driving capability".
- We have three sub-classes: OffRoadVehicle, SportVehicle and PassengerVehicle, each of which extends the Vehicle class.
1. PassengerVehicle class: It's using the drive() method from the Vehicle class, which means it inherits the normal driving capability.
2. OffRoadVehicle class: It overrides the drive() method to provide its own implementation, which returns "Sport Driving capability".
3. SportVehicle class: It also overrides the drive() method to provide its own implementation, which returns "Sport Driving capability".
// This design has the following issues:
- And both 'OffRoadVehicle' and 'SportVehicle' sub-classes have a common functionality of driving the vehicle in drive() method i.e. which returns "Sport Driving capability".
- Here, we are using duplicate driving capability in both of these sub-classes.
- This is a violation of the DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) principle, as we are repeating the same code in multiple places.
- We will solve this by using the Strategy Pattern.